# What do you think about AI?

AI is a great tool for programmers. With AI I can implement a feature in 10 minutes that would normally take like 10 days. It is a revolution; redefines how coding works. It becomes an industry standard, it is now a part of everyone's life.

# What worked well and what didn't?

I feel like, a problem of AI is that it is an expert at making small softwares that works, but it is difficult to make long term, maintainable, and scalable softwares. Using AI to start a project is very quick, but iteration is a nightmare. Afterall you still need a human programmer who actually understands the architecture and can guide the AI when it fails.

Another problem I feel like is that AI always claims it can do something that it actually cant't - it confidently says "yes I can do this" and then produce something that is completely unusable.

My feeling is, if you have an idea that you know it works, and you have an architecture that you know is capable, the AI is your mightiest sword. It can implement your design faster than any human can do. But, if you know nothing about your actual design, you just give your idea to AI and ask it to decide itself, then... it might work, but it might not. AI works best if I give it an architecture and implementation plan. Vibe-coding works fine for small and single-use projects, but it becomes difficult at larger projects that you want to use for a long time.

# How do you use AI

I am not satisified with a default codex or something. I am customizing it; I am finding its full potential. I connected it to my calendar, to my school email, to my school course websites, and to all the resources. It helps me summarizing content, pointing out action items, and guide me to find the note I want. It becomes a part of my everyday life.

I am also developing my own MCP servers. For example, I connected Codex to minecraft, now it can help me make Minecraft films. For so long AI can only make Minecraft films in a fake renderer or something, but now it can go into a real MInecraft world, build stuff, and create films based on real Minecraft blocks.

I would say we should not be limited by what AI "currently" can do. Just in 2026, we all see how AI is rapidly developed. Agentic coding was still a new thing in 2025 and few people actually embraced it. But in 2026, it already becomes a tool that everyone use. I can't imagine what AI can do in 2027.
